# Line endings, so `format:check` gives the same answer on every machine.
#
# ── THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Prettier is configured with `endOfLine: "lf"`, and CI runs `prettier --check .`
# on Linux, where a checkout is LF and it passes. On Windows, `core.autocrlf` is
# commonly `true`, so the same checkout arrives as CRLF and prettier objects to
# **almost every file in the repository**.
#
# The local signal is then so noisy it stops being read. This project has the
# scar: `format:check` was written off as "unusable on Windows" and replaced in
# practice by narrowed globs — `prettier --check "src/**/*.{ts,tsx}"` — which
# quietly check nothing outside those globs. That went unnoticed until a
# hand-written `vercel.json` broke `main`, because JSON was not in the glob.
#
# A guard that is too noisy to read is not a guard. This makes the working tree
# LF everywhere, so `npm run format:check` locally is the same command with the
# same result as CI, and there is no reason left to narrow anything.
#
# ── WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT CHANGE ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing in the repository's content. Measured before writing this, with
# `git ls-files --eol` rather than by inspection: of 477 tracked files, 35 are
# binary and 4 are empty; of the 438 text files that remain, 436 are ALREADY
# stored LF and the 2 exceptions are excluded below. This only changes what
# lands in a *working tree* on checkout.
#
# Windows developers will see files rewritten once, on the next checkout or
# after `git add --renormalize .`. That is the point, and it happens once.

# Normalise text in the repository, and check it out with LF everywhere —
# including on Windows, overriding core.autocrlf.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── The two exceptions ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The researchers' knowledge-base export and the eval history are written by a
# spreadsheet, which produces CRLF. Both are stored that way today.
#
# `-text` leaves them exactly as they are, in the repository and in the working
# tree. Normalising them would be a content change to data files nobody asked
# to change, and every future export would flip them back — a diff that appears
# on its own and teaches people to ignore diffs. Python's `csv` module reads
# either without caring.
*.csv -text

# Binary, stated explicitly. `text=auto` detects these correctly on its own;
# saying so removes the chance of a heuristic being wrong about a font or an
# image and corrupting it with line-ending conversion.
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.woff2 binary
*.woff binary
*.ttf binary
*.ico binary
*.mp3 binary
*.pdf binary
